   "format": "mcq",
   "stem": {
      "text": "Let \\( y = f(x) \\) be the solution to the differential equation \\( \\frac{dy}{dx} = x^{2} + y \\) with \\( f(-1) = 2 \\). Find the approximation to \\( f(0) \\) given by Euler's method, starting at \\( x = -1 \\) with two steps of equal size. Give the exact value."
   },
   "answer_key": {
      "form": "symbolic",
      "mathjson": [
         "Rational",
         43,
         8
      ]
   },
   "worked_solution": [
      {
         "step": 1,
         "text": "The step size is \\( \\Delta x = \\frac{0 - (-1)}{2} = \\frac{1}{2} \\).",
         "mathjson": [
            "Rational",
            1,
            2
         ]
      },
      {
         "step": 2,
         "text": "At \\( (-1, 2) \\) the slope is \\( 3 \\), so \\( y \\approx 2 + (\\frac{1}{2})(3) = \\frac{7}{2} \\) at \\( x = - \\frac{1}{2} \\).",
         "mathjson": [
            "Rational",
            7,
            2
         ]
      },
      {
         "step": 3,
         "text": "At \\( (- \\frac{1}{2}, \\frac{7}{2}) \\) the slope is \\( \\frac{15}{4} \\), so \\( y \\approx \\frac{7}{2} + (\\frac{1}{2})(\\frac{15}{4}) = \\frac{43}{8} \\) at \\( x = 0 \\).",
         "mathjson": [
            "Rational",
            43,
            8
         ]
      },
      {
         "step": 4,
         "text": "So \\( f(0) \\approx \\frac{43}{8} \\).",
         "mathjson": [
            "Rational",
            43,
            8
         ]
      }
